/// Codex's trust prompt, captured the same way on the same day by
/// `bash scripts/check-trust-prompts.sh --save`.
///
/// Its yes comes first and already carries the cursor, so the right answer here
/// is `Enter` alone — the opposite of Claude Code, which is the whole reason a
/// fixed key sequence cannot be right for both. The first line is the trap this
/// one contributes: `> You are in …` starts with a cursor glyph and is not an
/// option, so a parser that looks for the cursor before it looks for the
/// options counts from the wrong row.
const CODEX_TRUST_PROMPT_PANE: &str = concat!(
    "> \x1b[1mYou are in \x1b[0m/private/var/folders/29/1fj7pwg51hb3g8s6lyy_rldw945t4y/T/operon-trust-probe.7XHsP5\n",
    "\n",
    "  Do you trust the contents of this directory? Working with untrusted contents comes with higher risk of prompt injection. Trusting the directory allows project-local config, hooks, and exec policies\n",
    "  to load.\n",
    "\n",
    "\x1b[38;5;6m› 1. Yes, continue\x1b[39m\n",
    "  2. No, quit\n",
    "\n",
    "  \x1b[2mPress enter to continue\x1b[0m\n",
    // the rest of the 51-row pane, blank.
    "\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
);

